  <div class="section-label">4000 South Terminus</div>
  <h2>Interchange Configuration at 4000 South</h2>

  <p>The 4000 South endpoint represents the southern terminus of the Weber County extension segment. Final traffic modeling will determine whether this terminus is configured as a <strong>grade-separated interchange</strong> (overpass/underpass with ramps) or an <strong>at-grade signalized intersection</strong>.</p>

  <p>A full grade-separated interchange typically consumes 15–25 acres of land and generates substantial feeder traffic on adjacent local streets. An at-grade intersection has a smaller footprint but limited long-term capacity. The configuration will be determined during final traffic modeling in the SES.</p>

  <div class="section-label">Environmental Considerations</div>
  <h2>Wetlands, Water Resources & Ecological Impacts</h2>

  <p><strong>Wetlands.</strong> The Phase 2 Davis County extension (which precedes Phase 3) identified approximately 61.9 acres of wetlands in its updated environmental study (2025–2026), representing a 34% increase from the 2017 EIS estimate of 46.1 acres.{% cite "udot-wdc-phase2-noise" %} Wetlands are protected under federal and state law and require mitigation when impacted. Common mitigation approaches include:</p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li><strong>On-site restoration or creation</strong> of replacement wetlands</li>
    <li><strong>Off-site mitigation</strong> through wetland banking</li>
    <li><strong>Alignment adjustment</strong> to avoid sensitive areas</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The Phase 3 Weber County area (near Hooper) includes agricultural land and areas near the Great Salt Lake lakebed, which may contain wetlands. The Draft SES (expected Fall 2026) will identify specific wetland impacts and mitigation strategies.</p>

  <p><strong>Groundwater and Drainage.</strong> Western Weber County's soft soils and high groundwater reflect its location on the former Great Salt Lake lakebed. The SR-177 construction includes extensive drainage infrastructure coordinated with the Hooper Water Improvement District. Property owners in the immediate corridor should be aware that:</p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Groundwater levels may fluctuate during and after construction</li>
    <li>Drainage patterns may change, affecting adjacent properties</li>
    <li>Well water quality or quantity could be affected for nearby property owners</li>
    <li>Irrigation systems (agricultural use) may require adjustment</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Agricultural and Ecological Resources.</strong> The Hooper area is known for its agricultural heritage and open space. The Phase 3 alignment will impact agricultural lands, irrigation infrastructure, and habitat. Specific ecological impacts (wildlife corridors, native plant communities, species of concern) will be detailed in the SES environmental analysis. Property owners with agricultural operations should anticipate:</p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Land fragmentation (highways dividing agricultural parcels)</li>
    <li>Loss of agricultural productivity</li>
    <li>Changes in water availability for irrigation</li>
    <li>Severance damages (reduced value of remaining land)</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Air Quality.</strong> During construction, air quality impacts include dust generation, equipment emissions, and construction traffic. Mitigation typically includes dust control measures, emission standards for equipment, and traffic management. Post-construction, operational-phase emissions from increased freeway traffic (primarily vehicles, some heavy trucks) will be modeled in the SES. Properties within 500 feet of the corridor may experience:</p>
  <ul style="margin: 10px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 1.8;">
    <li>Increased particulate matter and vehicle emissions</li>
    <li>Odors from heavy vehicle traffic</li>
    <li>Potential health impacts for sensitive populations (children, elderly, those with respiratory conditions)</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="section-label">Geotechnical Challenges</div>
  <h2>Soft Soils and High Groundwater</h2>

  <p>The soft soils and high groundwater levels characteristic of western Weber County — the former lakebed environment near the Great Salt Lake — necessitate advanced geotechnical engineering. Earlier West Davis bridge and structure designs provide a useful precedent for these conditions, including support approaches such as <strong>integral bents</strong>, which distribute forces across the structure rather than concentrating them at discrete footings.{% cite "hntb-geotech" %}</p>

  <p>Construction in this segment is a multi-phase process including:</p>

  <ul style="margin: 16px 0 16px 24px; line-height: 2;">
    <li>Site preparation and clearing</li>
    <li>Drainage installation (coordinated with the Hooper Water Improvement District)</li>
    <li>Foundation engineering for soft-soil conditions</li>
    <li>Infrastructure relocation (Rocky Mountain Power, Davis &amp; Weber Counties Canal Company, Hooper Irrigation Company)</li>
    <li>Paving and surface work</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The Phase 2 Davis County extension provides direct precedent: utility relocations began December 2025,{% cite "se-extension-davis-start" %} with full construction launching Summer 2026{% cite "udot-2026-construction" %} and public construction forecasts pointing to late 2028 or early 2029 completion.{% cite "cc-construction-forecast" %}</p>

  <div class="section-label">Multi-Modal Connections</div>
  <h2>Trail Extension and Active Transportation</h2>

  <p>The project includes the extension of the Emigrant Trail from West Point through to the Weber County line, eventually connecting to existing trails on <strong>5500 West</strong>.{% cite "udot-wdc-overview" %} The Phase 2 Davis County extension will extend the trail from 1300 North to connect to the existing 5500 West trail in Weber County.</p>

  <p>West Haven City's Master Park Plan (Resolution 09-2026){% cite "whc-res-09-park-plan" %} aims to leverage these trail investments to enhance local connectivity and recreational access. Preliminary utility relocations from Rocky Mountain Power and Hooper Water began along the southern edge of West Haven in Summer 2026 — infrastructure work for Phase 2, not the Weber County segment, but an indicator of the corridor's northward progression.</p>
